<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nero Claudius Drusus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">38–9 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Drusus the Elder, Drusus Germanicus · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julio-claudian-dynasty">Julio-Claudian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nero Claudius Drusus, known as Drusus the Elder, was a Roman general, stepson of the emperor Augustus and younger brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a>. From 12 to 9 BCE he led campaigns across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> against the Sugambri, Chatti, Cherusci and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanic-peoples">Germanic peoples</a>, reaching the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>, and he dug a canal and sailed along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> coast. He died in 9 BCE after a fall from his horse and was given the posthumous title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanicus">Germanicus</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nero-claudius-drusus.jpg" alt="Nero Claudius Drusus" width="320" height="462" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Siren-Com · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Drusus_l%27ancien_Rome_augGP2014.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Drusus was the younger son of Livia Drusilla and her first husband, Tiberius Claudius Nero, who was legally declared his father before the couple divorced. He was born between mid-March and mid-April 38 BCE, three months after Livia married Augustus. Rumours that Augustus was the real father arose, though Livia was already pregnant when she met him. Drusus was raised in his legal father's house with his brother Tiberius, and the two remained close. He married Antonia Minor, the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a> and Augustus' sister Octavia, and they had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanicus">Germanicus</a>, Claudius, a daughter named Livilla and at least two children who did not survive childhood.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 19 BCE Drusus was allowed to hold public offices five years before the minimum age. As quaestor he fought Raetian raiders in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, and in late 15 BCE he became governor of the three Gaulish provinces, with his base at Lugdunum. There he founded the council of the Gaulish provinces, and in 10 BCE he inaugurated the altar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and Augustus at Condate.</p><p>From 14 BCE he built military bases along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and allied with the Batavi. In 12 BCE he sailed from near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nijmegen">Nijmegen</a> along canals built for the purpose to the mouth of the Ems, where the Chauci accepted Roman supremacy. In 11 BCE he ascended the Lippe, defeated the Tencteri and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usipetes">Usipetes</a> and survived a Cheruscan ambush at Arbalo, after which his men acclaimed him imperator. In 10 BCE he drove back the Chatti and Sicambri. As consul in 9 BCE he led the army through the lands of the Marsi and Cherusci and crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>, where he set up a trophy. The sources suggest he may have won the spolia opima, but he died before any ceremony.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On the return from the Elbe Drusus fell from his horse. He survived the fall, but infection set in and he died about a month later, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> at his side. His ashes were placed in the Mausoleum of Augustus. The Senate granted him the hereditary title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanicus">Germanicus</a>, which passed to his sons and was later borne by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caligula">Caligula</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a>. His soldiers raised the Drususstein at Mogontiacum, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>, and parts of it still stand.</p><p>Historian Michael McNally considers him the ablest of the Roman commanders who tried to conquer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania">Germania</a>. His death slowed Roman expansion, and after the loss of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-quinctilius-varus">Publius Quinctilius Varus</a>'s army in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-teutoburg-forest">Battle of the Teutoburg Forest</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> became the de facto border of the empire. When his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> became emperor in 41 CE, new honours were voted to Drusus, including annual games on 14 January.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nero Claudius Drusus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They appear in Roman sources around the time of <b>Drusus</b>' campaigns, and were at times allies, at times enemies of Rome.</blockquote><a href="/state/angrivarii">Angrivarii</a></li><li><blockquote>They appear in Roman sources from 12 BCE, when <b>Drusus</b> fought them on the Ems.</blockquote><a href="/state/bructeri">Bructeri</a></li><li><blockquote>They appear in Roman sources around 10 BCE, when <b>Drusus</b> campaigned against them.</blockquote><a href="/state/chatti">Chatti</a></li><li><blockquote>They enter Roman history in 12 BCE, when <b>Drusus</b> led a naval expedition along their coast and made them allies.</blockquote><a href="/state/chauci">Chauci</a></li><li><blockquote>After the campaigns of <b>Drusus</b> from 12 BCE they became Roman allies, and their noble sons, among them Arminius, served in the Roman army and received Roman citizenship.</blockquote><a href="/state/cherusci">Cherusci</a></li><li><blockquote>They became tributaries of Rome after the campaigns of <b>Drusus</b> in 12 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/frisii">Frisii</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania">Germania</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-20" aria-label="Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Nero Claudius Drusus"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5788 3947h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5788 3947h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-20">What was here in 20 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
